WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.721
A customer taps Pay, the network drops, the app retries, and the card is charged twice. Idempotency keys prevent exactly that.

00:00:11.921 --> 00:00:12.850
Pause and think.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.630
Where must the idempotency key be checked?

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:23.344
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.643
Networks fail in the middle of requests. Idempotency makes it safe to try again.

00:00:30.993 --> 00:00:32.862
Think about an elevator call button.

00:00:33.312 --> 00:00:39.768
Pressing it five times does not call five elevators. The result of one press and five presses is the same.

00:00:40.218 --> 00:00:43.352
A payment API should behave like that button.

00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:57.558
New word: A P I. Application Programming Interface: the agreed set of functions or requests one piece of software offers another. Picture it like this: A restaurant menu: you order from it without knowing how the kitchen works.

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:05.797
The client attaches an idempotency key, a unique ticket number, and the server remembers the result for that ticket.

00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:17.702
New word: idempotency key. A unique ID sent with a request so the server can recognise and ignore a retry. Picture it like this: A raffle ticket number: the same ticket can only win once.

00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:22.032
Here is the dangerous case.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:26.604
The client sends charge, with key k1. The server charges the card.

00:01:27.054 --> 00:01:32.220
The reply is lost on the way back, so the client times out and retries with the same key.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:38.231
The server sees k1 already done and returns the saved result. No second charge.

00:01:39.581 --> 00:01:41.996
Retry rules that interviewers check.

00:01:42.446 --> 00:01:48.205
Reuse the same key for every retry of one logical request. A new key means a new charge.

00:01:48.655 --> 00:01:56.933
Retry only on timeouts, connection errors, too many requests and five hundred errors, never on four hundred errors like bad input.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.970
Back off exponentially with jitter, and cap the attempts.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:07.974
Make writes idempotent with a key, retry only safe failures, and back off with jitter.

00:02:07.966 --> 00:02:09.220
Back to the question.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.000
Where must the idempotency key be checked?

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.337
On the server, atomically, before doing the work.

00:02:16.537 --> 00:02:17.408
Quick recap.

00:02:17.808 --> 00:02:19.004
Retries are normal.

00:02:19.404 --> 00:02:22.004
Idempotent means twice equals once.

00:02:22.404 --> 00:02:24.541
Use keys and backoff with jitter.
